WEBVTT

00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:04.472
When a graph behaves badly, debug one layer at a time.

00:00:04.472 --> 00:00:11.632
Log the current query, retrieved document I D's, grade or score, selected route, and step count.

00:00:11.632 --> 00:00:17.949
Inspect what each node received and the partial update it returned.

00:00:18.581 --> 00:00:23.968
Then inspect the router. Can every intended route actually be returned?

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:27.933
Do the route labels exactly match the graph mapping?

00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:33.293
If the graph loops, ask whether each cycle changes state meaningfully.

00:00:33.293 --> 00:00:35.808
Does the rewritten query change?

00:00:35.808 --> 00:00:41.492
Do different documents return? Keep the recursion limit as a hard safety net.

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:46.170
But do not use the recursion limit as your main stopping policy.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:51.766
A well-designed workflow should reach an explicit generate or stop condition on its own.
